$DIR/main.p:9:26: p holds a view of v (v.Ptr(0), line 8), which may have been passed to v.Push(i), which may free what it looks at or move it out (rt.MoveValues(&v.data[0], &old[0], len(old)), vec.p:434) at line 11, but the call deferred at line 9 uses it when the function returns
    p looks at memory that v owns, without owning it: the view was made by
    v.Ptr(0) at line 8. A view of an owner held in a local variable may be used
    only while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. A call that
    may free memory the view looks at, or move an owner out of it, through a
    pointer to the variable or to memory it owns ends the view as well. At line
    11 v was passed to v.Push(i), which may free what it looks at or move it out
    (rt.MoveValues(&v.data[0], &old[0], len(old)), vec.p:434) on some paths to
    this point, so the view may look at memory that was released, or at a value
    that now belongs to something else. The call deferred at line 9 keeps what p
    held there and runs when the function returns, after the deferred calls made
    after it (section 12.19).
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use p before line 11, or make that call only after the last use of p
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
